<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="e06898bf">&lt;p&gt;
    This task is important to determine whether a long term program/project is on track to realize its benefits. For
    example, if a program/project is not on track to realize benefits in Year 1 (of a five year business case), then there
    may be further actions that the program needs to consider to get back on track before the BAU handover can begin.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="ae4c5e89"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="1f278380"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="2b8714a5">&lt;p&gt;
    After&amp;nbsp;Benefits Realization&amp;nbsp;process is standardized, the benefits realization tasks will be transitioned to
    the business operations.&amp;nbsp;The operations will continue the established schedule of benefits monitoring, periodic
    updates to the sponsoring executives and steering committees, and implementation of corrective actions if scorecards
    are not tracking to plan.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    To drive sustainable change the following tools could be leveraged:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Implementation &amp; continuity planning
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Competency alignment / adjustment
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Knowledge transfer
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    It is at this point of time where most transformation fail and this task cannot be overstressed. This task ensures that
    the benefit tracking and reporting is fully transitioned as a Business As Usual (BAU) process.&amp;nbsp;It should be very
    clear to the transition team where, how and frequency of data gathering and reporting.&amp;nbsp;The operating team will
    also have to ensure that they leverage the data driven analytics approach&amp;nbsp;and how the various drivers will
    influence the final benefit outcome.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
